{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.CoshCosSeparation","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.CoshCosSeparation","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Trigonometric.Deriv","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Trigonometric.Basic"],"declarations":5,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.cos_le_one_le_cosh","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.cos_le_one_le_cosh","kind":"theorem","line":95,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/CoshCosSeparation.lean#L95-L98","doc":"`cos x ≤ 1 ≤ cosh x`, the sign asymmetry the separation rests on: the quadratic\nterm enters `cosh` with `+` and `cos` with `−`, so `1` always sits between them. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.cosh_div_lt_one_lt_cos_div_scaled","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.cosh_div_lt_one_lt_cos_div_scaled","kind":"theorem","line":85,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/CoshCosSeparation.lean#L85-L93","doc":"The scaled form, as the cheat sheet uses it: with integer channel indices `p < r`\nand a positive substrate parameter `η` small enough that `r·η` stays inside `(0, π/2)`,\nthe two candidate readings of the same ratio bracket `1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.cosh_div_lt_one_lt_cos_div","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.cosh_div_lt_one_lt_cos_div","kind":"theorem","line":73,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/CoshCosSeparation.lean#L73-L83","doc":"**The separation.** For any two arguments strictly inside `(0, π/2)`, the\nhyperbolic ratio is below `1` and the trigonometric ratio is above it.\n\nThis is the statement that refutes reading a sub-percent residual as \"the cosh-vs-cos\ndiscrepancy\": the two forms are not near each other, they are on opposite sides of\n`1`, uniformly in the parameter. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.one_lt_cos_div_cos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.one_lt_cos_div_cos","kind":"theorem","line":61,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/CoshCosSeparation.lean#L61-L71","doc":"A ratio of `cos` at increasing arguments in `[0, π/2)` is above `1`.\n\nThe hypothesis `b < π/2` is what keeps the denominator positive; past `π/2` the ratio\nchanges sign and the statement is not the interesting one. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.cosh_div_cosh_lt_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.cosh_div_cosh_lt_one","kind":"theorem","line":52,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/CoshCosSeparation.lean#L52-L59","doc":"A ratio of `cosh` at increasing nonnegative arguments is below `1`.\n\nStated with `|a| < |b|` because that is the form `Real.cosh_lt_cosh` takes — `cosh` is\neven, so it is the magnitudes that order it. ","signatureChunk":0}]}